-- title:
--   Order of j at places above j=0 divides 12/(p-1)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $p<5$, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number with $p\nmid M$, and let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$. Consider the intermediate field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) $\Omega$ $M$ of the field of Laurent series $\Omega((q))$, namely `qExpFunctionFieldC` for the group $\Gamma_1(M)$, that is the $\Omega$-subfield generated by the ratios `intFormRatiosC` attached to $\Gamma_1(M)$. Let $J$ be an element of this field whose underlying Laurent series is [`ModularCurve.jqModC`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) $\Omega$, i.e. $q^{-1}$ times the image in $\Omega[[q]]$ of the integral power series `jNum` $=E_4^3\cdot\eta^{-24}$-type numerator, so $J$ is the reduction of the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$. Let $x$ be a place of this function field over $\Omega$ in the sense of [`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22): a valuation subring, distinct from the whole field, containing the image of $\Omega$ and a principal ideal ring; and let $x.\mathrm{ord}$ be the associated normalised integer valuation ($-\log$ of the adic valuation). Assume $0<x.\mathrm{ord}(J)$. Then $x.\mathrm{ord}(J)$ divides the integer $12/(p-1)$, computed with natural-number division and truncated subtraction, hence $12$ when $p=2$ and $6$ when $p=3$.
--
--   The places at which $\mathrm{ord}(J)>0$ are those lying over $j=0$, and the assertion bounds the ramification of the $j$-line map at such places in characteristic $2$ and $3$, where $\mathrm{Aut}(E_0)/\{\pm1\}$ has order $12$ resp. $6$; no condition $M\ge 5$ is imposed. It feeds the count of places over $j=0$ used in the lower bound for the number of supersingular points, in particular in [`ModularCurve.sub_one_mul_index_gamma1_le_twelve_mul_natCard_evalAt_mem_ssJSet_x1FunctionFieldC`](thm.html#ModularCurve.sub_one_mul_index_gamma1_le_twelve_mul_natCard_evalAt_mem_ssJSet_x1FunctionFieldC).
